{"qid":"Q191","names":["Estonia","Republic of Estonia"],"title":"Estonia","endonym":{"native":"Eesti Vabariik","lang":"et"},"capsule":"The first Republic of Estonia declared independence from Russia in February 1918 and secured it militarily in the War of Independence (1918–1920), recognized by Soviet Russia in the Treaty of Tartu (1920). Through the 1920s Estonia enacted sweeping land reform and developed oil-shale industry; the 1925 cultural autonomy law became a model for minority rights in Europe. Konstantin Päts's 1934 coup ended parliamentary rule, replacing it with a presidential-authoritarian system until new elections in 1938; the Nazi-Soviet Pact of August 1939 assigned Estonia to the Soviet sphere, and Soviet occupation and annexation followed in June–August 1940.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Estonia's interwar territory is precisely defined by the 1920 Treaty of Tartu and well documented; the period 1919–1940 is thoroughly attested.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Estonia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Estonia"},{"label":"Britannica: Estonia — History","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Estonia/History"},{"label":"Britannica: Konstantin Päts","url":"https://www.britannica.com/biography/Konstantin-Pats"}],"britannica":"place/Estonia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Estonia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q887019","name":"Otto Strandman","start":1919,"end":1919,"image":"Otto Strandman (1-E-2966).jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Otto%20Strandman%20(1-E-2966).jpg?width=320","imageLicense":{"license":"Public domain","artist":"Unknown author","attr